```
@id("allow-readonly-lookups")
permit (
  principal,
  action in [
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder",
    Drupe::Action::"list_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_failure_codes",
    Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval"
  ],
  resource
);

@id("close-main-only-after-human-signoff")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"get_workorder"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.approval_outcome: "SIGNED_OFF",
    output.approver_type: "OPERATOR"
  }
};

@id("never-approve-own-work")
forbid (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"approve_workorder", resource);
```

I based "approved by a person" on `get_workorder`'s `approval_outcome` and `approver_type` fields, because they have fixed enum values. A close is allowed only if a completed `get_workorder` call for the same `wonum` and `site_id` returned `SIGNED_OFF` from an `OPERATOR` within the last hour. A `DECLINED` or `QUEUED` outcome, or a `BOT` or `SCHEDULER` approver, does not count. The action schema declares the `authority_get_approval` fields `decision` and `approver_kind` only as free-text strings. I could not tell what values mean "approved" and "human", so I did not build the policy on them. That tool remains available for lookups.

The `get_workorder` permit is a plain one, so its calls are recorded as responses and can serve as evidence. A denied or failed call is recorded only as an error and cannot satisfy the condition.

The other tools (`update_workorder`, `assign_technician`, `cancel_workorder`, `generate_work_order`) have no permit and are denied by default. The `approve_workorder` forbid is there so no other policy can ever allow the agent to approve.

If a work order was signed off and then declined within the same hour, the earlier sign-off would still satisfy the condition. The temporal rules have no clean way to express "not later declined" here.
